# serial_cycle_succession -- the same ring with `beech` standing exactly
# where `birch` stood, and nothing else moved.
#
# This is the one repair a ring accepts. `serial_cycle_shadow.bron` failed
# because a standby present beside the domain it replaces is a second
# writer; `serial_cycle_bypass.bron` failed because a route drawn around a
# domain gives the shape a choice of laps and a ring has exactly one. Both
# refusals are about a document that names the standby WHILE the domain is
# still there.
#
# So this document names it AFTER. The regions are the ring's own four,
# byte for byte and name for name -- `ask_ab`, `ask_bc`, `ask_ca`, `taken`
# -- because a region belongs to the declaration rather than to a process,
# and every ask already standing in one is exactly where it was left.
# `beech` takes birch's grants unchanged: `ask_ab` to read, `ask_bc` to
# write. Every region still has one writer and one reader, every domain
# still spans two peers, and one lap still closes: alder to beech to cedar
# and home.
#
# What the succession does cost is the seam. A channel carries the names of
# its two ends, so `alder->birch` and `birch->cedar` are gone and
# `alder->beech` and `beech->cedar` are new, standing silent at zero. The
# ring alder already spent on birch is spent for good, and the successor
# must be woken again by name.
#
# Read whole by `caravan/read.rye`; carried home by `caravan/standby.rye`.

system serial_cycle_succession

domain alder
domain beech
domain cedar

channel alder beech
channel beech cedar
channel cedar alder

region ask_ab 4096
region ask_bc 4096
region ask_ca 4096
region taken 64

grant ask_ab alder rw
grant ask_ab beech r
grant ask_bc beech rw
grant ask_bc cedar r
grant ask_ca cedar rw
grant ask_ca alder r
grant taken alder rw
grant taken cedar r
